nt to be its own inverse.","It makes repeated products unambiguous.","It guarantees every operation is commutative.","It removes the need for closure."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b06cda61d579267cc846b19e","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch20_associativity_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Compute both sides for subtraction: what are $(10-5)-2$ and $10-(5-2)$?","options":["$3$ and $3$","$3$ and $7$","$7$ and $3$","$7$ and $7$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3fef228f190d7820ae3e7f1b","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch20_associativity_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For ordinary addition, what is $(2+3)+4$ compared with $2+(3+4)$?","options":["Both are $9$.","They are $5$ and $7$.","The second expression is undefined.","They are $20$ and $14$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eaa66d6573714c4d80f2c464","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"la_batch20_associativity_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For square matrices of the same size, what does associativity of matrix multiplication allow?","options":["Writing $ABC$ without choosing between $(AB)C$ and $A(BC)$.","Changing $ABC$ into $BAC$.","Multiplying rectangular matrices of any incompatible sizes.","Replacing every matrix product by entrywise multiplication."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"870ea6028b61daa178a78b93","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch20_associativity_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Why is the averaging operation $a\\star b=(a+b)/2$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ not associative?","options":["It has no two inputs.","It is associative because averages always lie between the inputs.","It is not closed on $\\mathbb{R}$.","For example, $(0\\star0)\\star4=2$ but $0\\star(0\\star4)=1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a306ca6eba299f039ac33d80","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"la_batch20_associativity_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In a noncommutative but associative operation, what remains safe?","options":["Both order and parentheses may always be changed.","Changing parentheses is safe, but changing the order may not be.","Changing the order is safe, but changing parentheses is not.","Neither order nor parentheses can ever be changed."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"09d668c733d5156d4f2c184e","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch20_associativity_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For exponentiation on positive real numbers, which comparison shows that it is not associative?","options":["Exponentiation has no two inputs.","$(2^3)^2=512$ and $2^{(3^2)}=512$.","$(2^3)^2=64$ but $2^{(3^2)}=512$.","$(2^3)^2=8$ but $2^{(3^2)}=9$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"843c571d924a6ac70e0a358b","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}